# Commit 1: JDBC support + SAP Cloud Integration naming/version/duplicate-ID fixes

**Branch**: `Work-on-Parameters-and-Remaining-Components`
**Scope**: `packages/compiler` (the `@cpi-ai/compiler` package)
**Audience**: this file is written for both future Claude sessions and human
contributors picking up this branch. It explains not just *what* changed but
*why* — the SAP errors that drove each fix and the evidence behind it — so
the reasoning doesn't have to be re-derived from scratch.

---

## 1. What this commit adds: JDBC support

The compiler previously had no JDBC adapter at all. This adds it, following
the same architecture as the existing HTTP/SOAP/SFTP/IDoc/OData adapters,
using a real SAP-exported JDBC iFlow ZIP as ground truth (not invented).

**New files**:
- `src/model/JdbcAdapter.ts` — JDBC adapter configuration (data source
  alias, connection/query timeouts, batch mode, batch operation). Receiver-
  only: SAP Cloud Integration has no JDBC sender (a database can't push a
  message into CPI).
- `src/model/JdbcCall.ts` — a **mid-flow** component, not just a flow-level
  sender/receiver. Real SAP flows call JDBC as a request-reply step in the
  *middle* of processing (query a DB, keep going), and a single iFlow may
  call more than one database. This models that: it produces a BPMN
  `<serviceTask activityType="ExternalCall">` and separately carries the
  `JdbcAdapter` config that `BpmnProcessMapper` uses to generate the
  matching receiver participant + `JDBC` messageFlow.
- `src/writer/ServiceTaskWriter.ts` — JDBC steps compile to
  `<bpmn2:serviceTask>`, not `<bpmn2:callActivity>` like other processing
  components, so this is a new (small, structurally identical) writer.

**Modified to wire it in**: `ComponentRegistry.ts`, `ComponentFactory.ts`
(`JdbcCall` component type, `JDBC` adapter type), `BpmnProcessMapper.ts`
(generates the companion participant + messageFlow for every `JdbcCall` in
the flow, with collision-safe IDs), `ProcessWriter.ts` (dispatches
`serviceTask` nodes to the new writer), `capabilities.ts`, `index.ts`.

**AI-facing contract**: the SQL query is never invented as an adapter
property — it's the message body, set by a preceding `ContentModifier`
(`wrapContent`), exactly matching how the real reference export works
(`CM_SetDBQuery` → `RR_OCEDB` in the source ZIP).

---

## 2. What broke in real SAP imports, and why

Generating a ZIP and getting `validate() => { valid: true }` was **not**
sufficient — several real SAP Cloud Integration import errors surfaced only
by actually testing the output. Each is captured here so it isn't
rediscovered the hard way again.

### 2a. "Channel Name should be valid XML NCName" / "Whitespace not allowed in Receiver name"

**Root cause**: adapter `.name` fields (e.g. `"HTTPS Receiver"`,
`"Query Customer DB"`) were written *directly* as the BPMN
`<messageFlow name="...">` attribute — SAP's "Channel Name" — which must be
a valid XML NCName (no whitespace, must start with a letter/underscore).

**Fix**: new `src/utils/XmlName.ts`, one shared utility:
- `toXmlTechnicalName(value, fallback)` — replaces whitespace and any
  NCName-invalid character with `_`, collapses repeats, prefixes `_` if the
  result would start with a digit, never returns empty (falls back to a
  caller-supplied safe default). Preserves word boundaries as underscores
  rather than concatenating words, so the output stays legible:
  `"Query Customer DB"` → `"Query_Customer_DB"`,
  `"Domestic Processing Service"` → `"Domestic_Processing_Service"`.
- `ensureUniqueTechnicalName(candidate, usedSet)` — deterministic dedup
  (`name`, `name_2`, `name_3`, ...) for when two different components
  produce the same technical name.
- `isValidXmlNCName(value)` — used by `validate()` as a regression guard.

Applied inside every adapter's `.sender()`/`.receiver()` factory
(`HttpAdapter`, `JdbcAdapter`, `SoapAdapter`, `SftpAdapter`, `IdocAdapter`,
`ODataAdapter` — the first fix only covered HTTP/JDBC; SOAP/SFTP/IDoc/OData
had the identical latent bug and were fixed once found). Component IDs from
AI-supplied JSON go through the same function via
`ComponentFactory.resolveComponentId()`.

**Important architectural point**: because sanitizing at N call sites is
fragile (a new adapter, or a mapper-level default, could still reach the
writer unsanitized), a **safety net was added at the single point where a
messageFlow name is actually serialized** — `CollaborationWriter.ts` now
runs every channel name through `toXmlTechnicalName()` one more time before
writing it, idempotently. This guarantees the invariant for the final XML
regardless of which upstream code produced the value.

**Also discovered live**: SAP rejects whitespace in the adapter's `Name`
*property* too (shown as the title in the General/Connection tab), not just
the technical channel name — this contradicted older internal documentation
(`VALIDATION_ERRORS_FIXED.md`) that said the `Name` property tolerated
spaces. Confirmed against a live tenant. Every adapter's `Name:` property
now reuses the same sanitized value as the channel name (no more
purposely-different "human-readable" copy for this field).

Participant `name=` attributes (the Sender/Receiver/Integration Process
boxes) were deliberately **left alone** — real SAP exports in this repo show
the `"Integration Process"` participant with a space in its name, so that
field is not NCName-constrained the way messageFlow `name` is.

### 2b. "This component HTTPS with version 1.5 is not supported in Cloud Integration profile"

**Root cause**: `HttpAdapter.receiver()` inherited `ComponentType` from the
requested `protocol`, so requesting an `"HTTPS"` receiver produced
`ComponentType=HTTPS`. SAP Cloud Integration has **no HTTPS-typed Receiver**
in its profile catalog — only Sender. The outbound adapter is always
`ComponentType=HTTP` (it accepts both `http://` and `https://` target URLs
via the URL itself).

**Fix**: `HttpAdapter.receiver()` now always produces
`ComponentType=HTTP`, `TransportProtocol=HTTP`, `componentVersion=1.16`,
`.../version::1.16.1` — evidence: `DISCOVERY_REPORT_PHASE3.md`,
"Receiver Metadata (HTTP)", captured from a real SAP export
(`IPRO_PRODUCT_HTTP.iflw`). The `protocol` config field is still accepted
(reads naturally when the target `url` is `https://`) but no longer changes
what's emitted.

### 2c. "This component JDBC with version 1.5 is not supported in Cloud Integration profile"

**Root cause**: diffing the generated JDBC messageFlow property-by-property
against the reference JDBC export showed one missing property: `Vendor:
"SAP"` — present in every real JDBC export, absent from this compiler's
output (HTTP/SOAP don't have this property, which is likely why it was
missed).

**Fix**: added `Vendor: "SAP"` to `JdbcAdapter`. The JDBC `componentVersion`
(`1.5`/`1.5.3`) was left unchanged — no evidence in this repo suggests a
different version, and it matches the reference export exactly. If a live
tenant's Cloud Integration profile has moved past that version, that would
need a newer reference export to confirm rather than guessing a number.

### 2d. "Enter a valid address, for example http(s)://example" on the HTTP receiver

**Root cause**: `HttpAdapter.receiver()` wrote the configured URL to a
`staticUrl` property. SAP's Address field does not read `staticUrl` at
all — it reads **`httpAddressWithoutQuery`** (+ `httpAddressQuery` for any
query string). This wasn't a guess: this repo's own
`V1.2.3a_ROUTER_VALIDATION_FIX.md`, from an earlier SAP validation cycle,
already documents *"HTTP adapter requires `httpAddressWithoutQuery` to be
configured"*, and the same property names already existed (unused by
`HttpAdapter`) in `BpmnProcessMapper.ts`'s older hardcoded default block.

**Fix**: new `splitAddressForHttpReceiver()` helper splits `config.url` into
`httpAddressWithoutQuery` (everything before `?`) and `httpAddressQuery`
(everything after, no leading `?`). Both properties are always present
(empty when unconfigured) to match the evidenced pattern. `staticUrl` was
removed.

**Also added**: `validate()` now emits a **warning** (`AD-008`, not an
error) when an HTTP receiver has no address configured — per the same
evidence, SAP treats this as an acceptable "configure later in the SAP UI"
state for template flows, not a structural failure, so it must not flip
`validate()` to invalid.

### 2e. CP-001 — "Duplicate component ID: ProcessCall_1786467900757"

**Root cause**: several `Component` subclasses generate their own ID via
`Date.now()` when none is supplied, which can collide when two instances of
the same type are constructed within the same millisecond. This had already
been fixed once for `ProcessCall` (accepts an optional `id` param), but
`XmlValidator`, `MessageMapping`, `Multicast`, `Splitter`, `Gather`, and
`XsltMapping` still had the bug.

**Fix**: added the same optional `id?: string` parameter (with the
`Date.now()` fallback kept, matching the existing pattern) to all six, wired
through `ComponentFactory.createComponent()`. More importantly, added
`ComponentFactory.resolveComponentId()`: every component created via
`fromJson()` now gets its final technical ID computed **once**, up front —
sanitized via `toXmlTechnicalName()` and deduplicated via
`ensureUniqueTechnicalName()` against every other component in the same
flow (main process, subprocesses, and exception subprocesses share one
`usedComponentIds` set). This closes the loophole for the actual AI-JSON
code path regardless of what any individual constructor's fallback does.

---

## 3. Validation hardening

`validate()` used to only check structure (sender/receiver presence, Router
route counts, component-ID uniqueness at the `IFlow` level) — never the
*generated adapter output*, which is why `valid: true` could still produce a
SAP-rejected ZIP. Added, in `src/api/validate.ts`:

- **NM-001** — channel name is not a valid XML NCName (regression guard;
  should be unreachable now that every adapter sanitizes at construction,
  but catches a future regression before it reaches SAP).
- **AD-006** — HTTP(S) sender address doesn't start with `/`.
- **AD-007** — adapter `(ComponentType, direction)` → `componentVersion`
  doesn't match the evidence-backed value this compiler is known to
  generate correctly (scoped to HTTP/HTTPS/JDBC — the two adapters with
  direct evidence of a wrong version; deliberately not extended to
  SOAP/SFTP/IDoc/OData without evidence).
- **AD-008** — HTTP receiver has no configured address (warning, not an
  error — see §2d).

These run against the *actual* `HttpAdapter`/`JdbcAdapter` instances
produced by `fromJson()`, including mid-flow `JdbcCall` adapters (which
aren't part of `getSender()`/`getReceiver()` and were previously invisible
to `validate()` entirely).

---

## 4. Files changed (this package only — root `src/` and Fiori/CAP untouched)

**New**:
- `src/model/JdbcAdapter.ts`
- `src/model/JdbcCall.ts`
- `src/writer/ServiceTaskWriter.ts`
- `src/utils/XmlName.ts`
- `scripts/inspectZip.ts` (dependency-free ZIP reader for the regression tests below)
- `test-jdbc-regression.ts`
- `test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts`
- `test-naming-version-regression.ts`
- `test-customer-flow-regression.ts`

**Modified**:
- `src/api/capabilities.ts`, `src/api/validate.ts`
- `src/factory/ComponentFactory.ts`
- `src/index.ts`
- `src/mapper/BpmnProcessMapper.ts`
- `src/model/HttpAdapter.ts`, `SoapAdapter.ts`, `SftpAdapter.ts`,
  `IdocAdapter.ts`, `ODataAdapter.ts`
- `src/model/XmlValidator.ts`, `MessageMapping.ts`, `Multicast.ts`,
  `Splitter.ts`, `Gather.ts`, `XsltMapping.ts`
- `src/model/DataStore.ts`, `src/model/IFlow.ts`
- `src/registry/ComponentRegistry.ts`
- `src/packager/IflowPackager.ts` (CRLF line endings — pre-existing fix,
  bundled in from this branch's earlier WIP state)
- `src/serializer/IflowSerializer.ts`
- `src/writer/CollaborationWriter.ts`, `ProcessWriter.ts`

**Deliberately not touched**: the Fiori UI, the CAP `downloadFlow`/service
layer (not part of this repository), and the root-level `src/` tree (a
separate, only-partially-synced mirror of `packages/compiler/src/` that
predates this work — see `COMPILER_PACKAGE_MIGRATION.md` for why it exists;
it was not updated to avoid conflating two different maintenance surfaces
in one commit).

---

## 5. Testing

Every fix above was verified against the **raw generated `.iflw` XML**, not
just `validate()`'s return value — `validate()` passing was explicitly
*not* treated as proof of correctness, since that was the original problem.

Regression suites (plain `ts-node` scripts — this repo has no Jest
installed despite some `*.test.ts` files using Jest syntax; see
`test-jdbc-regression.ts` etc. for the actual runnable pattern):
- `test-jdbc-regression.ts` — single + multiple JDBC instances, resource
  packaging, config validation (rejects unsupported properties / missing
  `dataSourceAlias` / Sender direction).
- `test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts` — full natural-language-shaped
  scenarios end to end (`fromJson → validate → compileToZip`).
- `test-naming-version-regression.ts` — the three required scenarios
  (simple HTTPS, HTTPS+JDBC, Router-based domestic/international routing
  matching the pre-existing `multiple-processcall-regression.test.ts`
  scenario), plus a dedicated duplicate-ID stress test and direct
  unit-checks of `toXmlTechnicalName()` against the exact examples above.
- `test-customer-flow-regression.ts` — pre-existing non-JDBC scenario,
  confirmed not to regress.
- `test/run-rt-003-tests.ts`, `test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts` —
  pre-existing Router/Mapping suites, confirmed not to regress.

Run with (from `packages/compiler/`):
```
npm run build   # or: npx tsc --noEmit
npx ts-node test-jdbc-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-naming-version-regression.ts
npx ts-node test-customer-flow-regression.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-rt-003-tests.ts
npx ts-node ../../test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts
```

---

## 6. Known limitations

- JDBC `componentVersion` (`1.5`/`1.5.3`) is pinned to the reference export
  evidence in this repo. If a target tenant's Cloud Integration profile has
  moved to a newer version, this needs a current SAP export or live-tenant
  check to confirm — not a guess.
- Two other adapter constructors accepted an `id`/name-sanitization pattern
  matching only what's evidenced in this repo; SOAP/SFTP/IDoc/OData version
  numbers were not re-validated against a live tenant (only their channel
  name/`Name` property sanitization was fixed).
- `ComponentFactory.createComponent()`'s `Date.now()` fallback (for the six
  component types fixed in §2e) remains as a safety net for direct
  model-class usage *outside* `fromJson()` — that code path has no shared
  "used IDs" set to dedupe against, same as before this change.
